Most developments in the chemistry and applications of Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) have been made possible thanks to the value of reticular chemistry in guiding the unlimited combination of organic connectors and secondary building units (SBUs) into targeted architectures. However, the development of new titanium-frameworks remains still limited by the difficulties in controlling the formation of persistent Ti-SBUs with predetermined directionality amenable to the isoreticular approach. Here we report the synthesis of a mesoporous Ti-MOF displaying a MIL-100 topology. MIL100(Ti) combines excellent chemical stability and mesoporosity, intrinsic to this archetypical family of porous materials, with photoactive ${\sf T i}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\sf-O})$ metal-oxo clusters. By using high-throughput synthetic methodologies, we have confirmed that the formation of this SBU is thermodynamically favored as is not strictly dependent on the metal precursor of choice and can be regarded as an adequate building block to control the design of new Ti-MOF architectures. We are confident the addition of a mesoporous solid to the scarce family of crystalline, porous titanium-frameworks available will be a valuable asset to accelerate the development of new porous photocatalysts without the pore size limitations currently imposed by the microporous materials available.
## Introduction
Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline, porous materials built from the interlinking of metal-oxo clusters with organic connectors.1 Their internal porosity can be engineered in size, shape and chemical function to render a family of crystalline solids with structural and functional diversities beyond compare. These intrinsic characteristics make MOFs appealing materials for modern storage, separation, delivery and catalysis technologies.2-6 The problems linked to the development of these applications have stressed the need of producing MOFs with superior chemical stability.7 Combination of hydrolytical stability with high surface area was first reported for sodalite-type frameworks built from imidazolate $(Z|{\mathsf{F}})^{8}$ or azolate9 connectors due to hydrophobic shielding and strong metal-nitrogen bonds. Control over the strength of the coordination bond is also key to the robustness of $Z r(N)$ and Hf(IV) MOFs.
Compared to these metals, titanium offers several advantages. It is less toxic, naturally more abundant and more adequate to endow these porous materials with photoactivity. Still, the synthesis of crystalline, porous titanium-organic frameworks remains comparatively underexplored.10 Whereas the discovery of the UiO family, based on $Z r_{6}{\cal O}_{4}({\cal O}{\sf H})_{4}({\sf R C O}_{2})_{12}$ clusters,11 boosted the synthesis of multiple MOFs from isostructural Zr and Hf metal-oxo clusters,12-14 the controllable synthesis of TiMOFs remains quite challenging yet. This is due to the higher reactivity of Ti precursors, more prone to hydrolysis, that facilitates the formation of amorphous titanium oxide in solvothermal conditions. The formation of titanium-oxocarboxylate clusters, capable of directing the formation of high symmetry, periodic architectures, demands a stricter control over the hydrolysis-condensation equilibria. These synthetic difficulties are possibly the reason for which almost all Ti-MOFs available are built from different secondary building units (SBUs). Figure 1 summarizes the diversity of Ti-SBUs reported for the limited number of MOFs available: $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{15}/\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{9}$ titanium-oxo clusters in MIL-177,15 $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{8}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ in MIL-125,16 $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ in PCN-22,17 $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6}$ in MOF- $901^{18}$ and MTM-1,19 $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{3}\mathsf{O}$ in COK-69,20 heterometallic clusters like $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{8}\bar{Z}\mathsf{r}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{12}$ in $\mathsf{P C N-415}^{21}$ and $\mathsf{T i}_{2}\mathsf{M}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ ( $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{a}$ , Mn) in MUV-10,22 or simple titanium nodes in NTU-923 and Ti-CAT-5,24 confirm the difficulties in targeting the formation of specific MOF architectures due to the difficulties in controlling the formation of persistent SBUs.
This lack of chemical control over the final material is arguably limiting further development, highlighting the importance of finding alternative synthetic strategies that make Ti-MOFs amenable to the design principles of isoreticular chemistry. One strategy used to gain control is to produce daughter frameworks by incorporation of titanium (IV) to pre-formed materials by partial post-synthetic replacement of parent metals. This was originally demonstrated for the UiO-66(Zr)25 and MOF-5(Zn) families.26 More recent examples describe the use of titanium (III) precursors followed by mild oxidation for partial doping of MOF-74, MIL-100 and PCN-333 with Ti.27 However, these methodologies do not allow for complete metal replacement and also suffer from poor control over the positioning of titanium atoms in the structure,28 which can result in spurious deposition of metal oxide coatings.29
Compared to this post-synthetic strategy, here we report the formation of MIL-100(Ti) by direct synthesis. Systematic exploration of the chemical space, by using high-throughput (HT) methodologies, has enabled identification of the optimal synthetic conditions to produce highly-crystallineV eswoAlirtdicsle fOrnolinme different titanium precursors. MIL-10D0O(IT:i1)0.1c0o39m/Cbi8nSeCs052t1h8Be chemical stability and mesoporosity, intrinsic to the MIL-100 family, with photoactive ${\sf T i}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\sf-O})$ metal-oxo clusters. Our work suggests that the formation of this SBU, classical in the chemistry of MOFs based in trivalent metals, is not strictly dependent on the metal source and might be used for the controlled design of new Ti-MOF architectures.
## Results and discussion
## Synthesis from pre-formed Metal Clusters
Our first approach to the direct synthesis of Ti-MOFs involved the use of titanium-oxo clusters to act as pre-formed SBUs. In principle, this was expected to favour the formation of crystalline solids under milder conditions than those conventionally used in MOF synthesis. This strategy has been successfully used to produce $Z{\boldsymbol{\ r}}(|{\mathsf{V}}),{\mathsf{\Sigma}}^{30}{\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{I V}}),{\mathsf{\Sigma}}^{31}$ and Fe(III)- MOFs32 by direct reaction of metal-oxo clusters with polyaromatic carboxylic acids. Compared to simple precursors, we also expected to minimize the precipitation of amorphous oxides due to the higher stability of the cluster against hydrolysis. Accordingly, we reacted as-made $\Gamma\mathrm{i}_{6}0_{6}(4-$ tbbz) $_{6}(0^{\mathrm{i}}\mathsf{P r})_{6}]$ (tbbz $=4$ -tert-butylbenzoic acid) clusters (Figure 2a) with trimesic acid $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c}}=$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid) between 80 and $160~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for multiple combinations of solvents (DMF, DEF, NMP, MeOH, EtOH….), metal to linker ratios, concentration and addition of different modulators like acetic or benzoic acid. We used a FLEX SHAKE high-throughput workstation from Chemspeed $\circledcirc$ for robotic dispensing of solids and liquids in order to optimize the screening of a broad range of synthetic conditions whilst ensuring reproducibility. All attempts were unsuccessful as we could only isolate unreacted cluster or amorphous solids for temperatures below or above ${100}^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ respectively. We argued this was due to the poor solubility of the cluster in these conditions and decided to investigate alternative mixture of solvents. After several tests, we observed that heating of a mixture of $[\mathsf{T i}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6}(4\mathsf{-t b}\mathsf{b}z)_{6}(\mathsf{O}^{\mathsf{i}}\mathsf{P r})_{6}]$ $(\Gamma\mathfrak{i}_{6})$ and $H_{3}b\mathsf{t c}$ in acetonitrile:tetrahydrofuran (ACN:THF, 3:1 $v/{\mathsf{v}}\%)$ led to the formation of a white, microcrystalline solid. In order to optimize the crystallinity of the material we varied systematically the temperature of the reaction, the addition of acetic acid as modulator, as well as the metal-to-linker ratio. We observed that, for a given Ti:btc ratio of 1:5, only unreacted cluster was obtained at temperatures below $140~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with no addition of modulator (Figures S1-S4). However, a crystalline solid is observed in presence of acetic acid as modulator (180- 550 equiv., $250\mathrm{-}750~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and temperatures above $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Note that a secondary crystalline phase is obtained as a side-product at low temperatures and high amounts of acetic acid (i.e. 550 equiv., $750\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ) as evidenced by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure S2-S5), whereas it is not observed for lower concentration of modulator. The formation of this secondary phase can be reduced by increasing the temperature of the reaction up to $160^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figures 2b-c and S6-S7). We next varied the Ti:btc ratio at $160^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for a fixed amount of acetic acid (i.e. 180 equiv., $250\upmu\mathrm{L})$ . PXRD of the solids confirm the formation of a highly crystalline MIL-100 phase for Ti:btc ratios 1:2-5 (Figure S8). PXRD of the solids synthesized with increasing temperatures and concentration of modulator confirm the critical effect of these parameters over the crystallinity of the material (Figures 2b-c). The optimum conditions for the formation of highly crystalline MIL-100(Ti) correspond to a Ti:btc ratio of 1:5 and addition of $250~\upmu\upiota$ of acetic acid (180 equiv.) reacted at $160^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (see ESI for details). The use of more conventional solvents like DMF or DEF yielded amorphous or poorly crystalline materials under equivalent reaction conditions (Figures S9-S10). Optical microscopy suggested the formation of small crystals, which was confirmed by Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM). As shown in Figure 2e, the bulk is composed of single crystals with an octahedral habit, typical of cubic MIL-100 phases, and sizes oscillating below $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . We also identified small fractions of amorphous material partially covering the surface of the crystals (Figure S11). The solid was recovered by centrifugation and washed with DMF and MeOH prior to drying in vacuum at room temperature.
## Structure and Characterization
PXRD pattern of the solid collected at our laboratory revealed clear similarities with the reported phases of MIL-100(Cr, Fe) (Figure S16).33,34 For a clearer structural description, we collected high-resolution PXRD data that was refined by using an starting model built from the reported structure of MIL100(Fe) by using Materials Studio. As shown in Figure 3a, Rietveld refinement of the activated solid converged with excellent residual values $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{w p}}=5.24\ \%)$ , $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{exp}}{=}1.93\ \%)$ for a cubic $F d-3m$ space group with cell parameters $a=73.5168(16),$ Å. The structure of MIL-100(Ti) is built from the interlinking of $\Tilde{\Pi}_{3}(\mu_{3^{-}}$ O) clusters and btc linkers to conform a zeolitic mnt topology that features two types of mesoporous cages with pentagonal and hexagonal pore windows (Figure 3b).
The assembly of this structure from the starting $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6}$ cluster used in the synthesis necessarily involves its degradation for the assembly of a thermodynamically more stable unit in the reaction conditions. This dynamic equilibrium between interconverting species is similar to that controlling the formation of heptameric clusters or Ti-oxo chains from $\mathsf{T i}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6}(\mathsf{a b}z)_{6}(\mathsf{O}^{\mathsf{i}}\mathsf{P r})_{6}$ ( $\mathtt{a b z}=4$ -aminobenzoate) during the synthesis of $P C N-22^{17}$ or DGIST-1,35 respectively. By using comparatively milder conditions, the integrity of the starting homo or heterometallic polynuclear Ti(IV) clusters can be also preserved and incorporated to the framework, as demonstrated for MOF901,18 MNT- $1^{19}$ or PCN-405.21 Compared to other precursors, these precedents seem to confirm the suitability of robust, molecular Ti clusters to either retain its nuclearity or control the formation of alternative SBUs more prone to induce the formation of specific MOF architectures under thermodynamic control. For example, $\mathsf{M}_{3}(\mu_{3}–0)$ $M=C r$ , Al, Fe) SBUs are very common in frameworks produced at relatively harsh conditions like MIL-9636 or MIL-101.37
The replacement of trivalent metals with Ti(IV) is not directly compatible with the local structure of MIL-100 materials, which feature 6-connected $[\mathsf{M}^{\mathsf{I I I}}]_{3}(\mu_{3}-\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{X})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}(\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c})_{6}]$ $({\mathsf{X}}^{-}={\mathsf{F}}.$ , Cl, OH) nodes. In our case, the introduction of an excess of one positive charge per metal atom shall be counterbalanced by additional anions. We hypothesized these could be directly coordinated to the Ti sites or occupying the empty space available from the mesoporous cages for neutral or cationic frameworks, respectively. FT-IR spectra of the solid, before and after Soxhlet washing (Figure S19), $^1\mathsf{H}$ -NMR (Figure S26) and solid-state $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}.$ CP-MAS-NMR (Figure S28), all discard the presence of $\Gamma\mathfrak{i}_{6}$ clusters or free trimesate or acetate anions occupying the pores. Hence, we modelled computationally different neutral clusters by modification of the molecules coordinated to the axial position of the octahedral Ti(IV) centers whilst fixing the positions corresponding to the btc linkers and the $\mu_{3}–0$ central bridge. We used the same methodology recently reported by De Vos and co-workers for rationalizing the most plausible formula of COK-69,20 based in analogue Ti(IV)-oxo trimers. We calculated the formation energies for the clusters that would generate a neutral structure by different combinations of $O^{2\cdot}$ -, ${\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ or ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ molecules acting as capping linkers. We optimized their structure with dispersion-corrected density functional theory (DFT-D3) as implemented in the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) (See S5 for calculation details). Our results suggests that the formation of the cluVisetweAr tic[lTeiO3(nlµin3e$\mathsf{O})\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{b t c})_{6}]$ (Figure 3c, left ) is 0.21DOeIV: 10m.1o0r3e9/fCa8vSoCu0r5a21b8lBe than $\left[\mathsf{T i}_{3}(\mu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{O})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{b}{\mathsf{t c}})_{6}\right]$ (Figure 3c, right). Nonetheless, like in the case of COK-69, the small difference between both clusters $(c.f.\ 0.4\ \mathrm{eV})$ is not sufficient to confirm unambiguously the formation of only one of them and both formulae might be reasonable.
The proposed formulae agree well with the percentage of residual oxide formed from the thermal decomposition of the solid (Figure S21). The experimental $32.2\%$ of $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ is quite close to the calculated value of $33.9\ \%$ . Permanent porosity was studied by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77~\mathsf{K}$ after activation of the solid under dynamic vacuum ( $10^{-3}$ mbar) at 150 $^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ overnight. MIL-100(Ti) displays a non-hysteretic, type-I $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption with two secondary gas uptakes at $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}=0.05$ and 0.14 (Figure 4a). The multi-point BET surface area was found to be $1321{\mathsf{m}}^{2}{\cdot}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ with a total pore volume of $0.66\mathsf{c m}^{3}.\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S22, Table S3). Pore Size Distribution (PSD) calculated by NonLinear Density Functional Theory (NLDFT) models reveals two types of mesoporous pores between 1.6-2.4 and $2.6{-}3.6~\mathsf{n m}$ that correlates well with the theoretical pore size values of 2.4 and $2.9\ \mathsf{n m}$ estimated from the structure of MIL-100(Ti). This surface area lies below the values reported for MIL-100(Cr, Fe) phases,33,34 suggesting ineffective activation or the partial blocking of the pores by molecular precursors with poor solubility. We used other activation protocols more adequate for highly porous materials like solvothermal treatment of the solid or soxhlet washing with hot EtOH or MeOH. None of them led to a substantial increase in porosity. As commented before,
FT-IR, $^1\mathsf{H}$ -NMR and $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ -CP-MAS-NMR of the activated solid rule out the presence of molecular components occluded in the pores of MIL-100(Ti). Thus, we ascribe the reduction of surface area to the formation of an amorphous fraction of the material that can be inferred from SEM (Figure S11), similar to previous reports for other mesoporous phases like MIL-101-NDC(Cr).38 ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption studies reveal a modest uptake of $1.70\:\mathrm{mmol\cdotg^{-}}$ 1 (7.5 wt $\%$ ) at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, with an isosteric heat of adsorption $(\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}})$ of 34.8 kJ·mol-1 (Figure 4b, S24). This value is very similar to the $30{-}35\ k\mathsf{J}{\cdot}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ reported for the Fe(III) phase39 and agrees well with the presence of vacant metal sites. Water adsorption also reveals clear similarities with other MIL-100 derivatives. As shown in Figure 4c, the isotherm at 298 K shows a $'\mathsf{S}^{\prime}$ -shape profile with hysteretic behaviour, indicative of irreversible capillary condensation. Water adsorption at low relative pressures, that can be ascribed to the presence of open metal sites in the structure, is followed by a steep uptake at relative humidity between 0.25 and $0.45\ \%$ with an inflection point at $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}=0.32$ , characteristic of hydrophilic materials.40 The maximum water adsorption capacity of MIL-100(Ti) calculated at $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}=0.9$ is $0.52{\bf g}{\cdot}{\bf g}^{-1}$ . This gravimetric adsorption is very similar to the value reported for MIL-100(Al) $(0.51\ \mathbf{g\cdotg^{-1}})$ 1 but smaller than the Cr and Fe phases ranging from 0.60 to 0.76 $\mathbf{g}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 4c, Table S4).41,42
## Thermal and Chemical Stability
One of the main features of MIL-100 materials is their stability in water. For Fe, Al and Sc phases, this stability is thermodynamic in origin and arises from strong coordination bonds with M-O bond dissociation energies of 444, 502 and 671 kJ·mol-1.43 In turn, the kinetic inertness of Cr(III) ions in MIL100(Cr) is responsible for its outstanding hydrolytical stability. Accordingly, the incorporation of highly charged Ti(IV) metals to MIL-100 topologies shall result in high thermal and chemical stability due to thermodynamic parameters. Compared to the Cr and Fe phases that decompose between $300{-}350~^{\circ}\mathsf C,33{,}34$ the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of MIL-100(Ti) confirms a significant increase in the thermal stability of the solid that remains stable up to $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Figure S21). This thermal stability is similar to other Ti-MOFs and agrees well with the higher dissociation energy of M-Ti(IV) bonds of 667 kJ.mol-1.43 We next evaluated the hydrolytical stability of MIL-100(Ti) by soaking freshly made solid in water under acidic and basic conditions $(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 1\mathsf{-}14)$ ). PXRD of the solids after incubation in water for 24 hours confirm that the structure of the solid remains unchanged between pH 2 and 12 (Figure 4d, S32). Stability was also confirmed by gas sorption. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements of the solids after water treatment show negligible changes in their surface area compared to the as-made material (Figure 4e and S33, Table S5). These results confirm that MIL-100(Ti) displays excellent hydrolytical stability, comparable to other benchmark materials.7
For further understanding on the role of $\bar{\mathsf{T i}}_{3}(\mu_{3}-\mathsf{O})$ nodes over the chemical stability of the material we decided to carry out equivalent experiments for direct comparison with the isostructural MIL-100(Fe)44 and a representative Ti-MOF like
MIL-125.16 PXRD and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ sorption confirm that reVpileawcAertimcle nOtn inoef Fe(III) with Ti(IV) centers does not inducDeOIi:m10p.1o0r3t9a/nCt8ScCh0a5n2g18eBs under equivalent conditions. MIL-100(Fe) only shows minor degradation of the structure at $\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 12$ (Figure S34) whereas its surface area is maintained close to the original value under acid and basic conditions (Figure S35, Table S6). This behaviour is different for MIL-125, that retains its structural integrity and porous function almost intact at pH-12 but undergoes significant degradation in acid pH (Figure S36, S37 and Table S7). We observe more important differences in the ICP-MS measurements of the supernatants after incubation in water. As shown in Figure 4f, titanium leaching in MIL-100(Ti) is almost negligible for acid and neutral conditions. In turn, the concentration of iron and titanium in solution increases with decreasing pH up to a maximum of 35 and $14~\mathrm{mg.mL^{-1}}$ at pH 2 for MIL-100(Fe) and MIL-125, respectively (see Table S8 for details). Concerning the isostructural series, we presume that the different degradation rates of the Ti and Fe phases might result in different drug delivery kinetics under simulated biological conditions. Although this study is beyond the scope of our work, we are confident MIL-100(Ti) might be of interest to the MOF community working in biomedical applications.
## Optical Response and Photoactivity
The applications of Ti-MOFs as photocatalysts for solar fuel production or light-induced organic transformations are pivotal to this family of materials due to the promising combination of high surface areas, chemical stability and photoactivity.45 The optical band-gap of MIL-100(Ti) obtained by Diffuse Reflectance Spectroscopy is $3.40~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S30). This value lies between those reported for MIL-125 $(3.68~\mathsf{e V})^{46}$ or MUV-10(Ca) (3.10 $\mathsf{e V})^{22}$ and is compatible with UV light photoexcitation. This was confirmed by irradiating a suspension of the solid in dry THF with UV-B light $(\lambda=280-315\mathrm{nm})$ . After a few hours, the color of the solid changed from white to dark blue (Figure S31) to remain stable in absence of oxygen. The Electron Paramagnetic Resonance (EPR) spectra of the solid before/after irradiation confirms the occurrence of a broad signal at $350~\mathsf{m T}$ , with $g_{^{-}}$ fitted parameters $\mathbf{g}_{\parallel}=\mathbf{1}.927$ and $\mathbf{g}_{\perp}=\mathbf{1}.952$ characteristic of Ti(III) species, only for the irradiated sample (Figure 5a, S31). This is accompanied by a weak, sharp peak at lower fields, that correspond to the formation of btc radicals with $\mathtt{g}\approx2.00$ .22 This behaviour is consistent with a ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMTC) mechanism, in which an electron is transferred from the photoexcited state of the linker to the metal sites (Figure 5b). We also tested the photocatalytic activity of MIL-100(Ti) by irradiating a suspension of the solid in a mixture of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{:M e O H}}$ (4:1, v/v %) with a Xe lamp (300 W) and measuring the ${\sf H}_{2}$ produced at different time intervals. As shown in Figure 5c, MIL100(Ti) shows an induction period of ca. 6 h for a total ${\sf H}_{2}$ production of $1000\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}$ after 24 hours (See S6 for Experimental Details). Stability was confirmed with PXRD and cyclability experiments for 5 consecutive cycles with minimum changes to the total ${\sf H}_{2}$ production measured (Figures S40-S41). The generation of photoinduced charge separation in MIL100(Ti), hallmark feature of semiconductors, was confirmed with Transient Absorption Spectroscopy (TAS). The decay kinetics profile of the solid suspended in ACN, measured at 500 nm, show two different processes. A fast component that decays during the first 500 ns, and a second one that remains stable in Ar or in presence of MeOH for approximately $20~\upmu s$ after the light pulse (Figure 5d). In addition, the longer-lived component is inhibited in presence of $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ suggesting that it likely originates from photogenerated Ti(III) species in the framework. This is further confirmed by the TAS spectrum recorded at 60 ns after the light pulse, that shows an absorption band centred at ca. $640\ \mathsf{n m}$ characteristic of Ti(III) species (Figure S42). The intensity of this band decreases after $6~\upmu s$ showing a broad absorption between $400{-}600\mathsf{n m}$ , likely due to the decay of an important fraction of the photogenerated species.
## Synthesis from other Metal Precursors
Previous works confirm the importance of choosing suitable titanium precursors for directing the synthesis crystalline titanium frameworks. We are confident the use of highthroughput synthetic methodologies might help studying this reactivity more systematically in order to enlarge the pool of precursors at hand. This pushed us to investigate the synthesis of MIL-100(Ti) by using other Ti salts with variable reactivity in water. We replaced $\Gamma\mathfrak{i}_{6}$ with precursors like $\mathsf{T i}(\mathsf{O}^{\mathsf{i}}\mathsf{P r})_{4},\mathsf{C p}_{2}\mathsf{T i C l}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{C p T i C l}_{3}$ and introduced minor changes to the synthesis optimized for the cluster. Our results confirm that MIL-100(Ti) can be prepared also from organometallic precursors. Whereas ${\mathsf{T i}}({\mathsf{O}}^{\mathrm{i}}{\mathsf{P r}})_{4}$ only afforded poorly crystalline solids for all the conditions explored (Figure 2d), PXRD LeBail refinement was used to confirm the phase purity of the solids formed with $\mathsf{C p}_{2}\mathsf{T i C l}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C p T i C l}_{3}$ . Full-profile fits yield excellent agreement factors and profile differences for both solids (Figure S17-S18). SEM analysis also confirms the formation of micrometric particles of sizes below $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ for $\mathsf{C p}_{2}\mathsf{T i C l}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C p T i C l}_{3}$ compared to the amorphous solid with ill-defined morphologies prepared with ${\mathsf{T i}}({\mathsf{O}}^{\mathsf{i}}{\mathsf{P r}})_{4}$ (Figure 2f-h, S12-13). Our experiments suggest that organometallic precursors are more adequate than the isopropoxide to induce the formation of crystalline MIL-100(Ti), likely due to their higher resistance against hydrolysis.
For a better understanding of the impact of the metal precursor over the properties of the MOF, we next analysed the porosity of MIL-100(Ti)- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{C p}}_{2}{\mathsf{T i C l}}_{2}$ with ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption experiments at $77~\mathsf{K}$
Compared to MIL-100(Ti)- $\cdot\mathsf{T i}_{6},$ the solid displays a similar isotherm and pore size distribution –indicative of to the formation of the same open framework– but a substantial decrease in the BET surface area and pore volume for $1130\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-}$ 1 and $0.51{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}\cdot{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure S23, Table S3). We argued this could be linked to the presence of bulkier Cp units replacing ${\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ as capping ligands for a concomitant decrease in porosity. The ability of cyclopentadienyl ligands to induce the formation of ${\mathsf{C p}}$ -capped titanium clusters was first anticipated for COK-69.20 To confirm this point we point we carried out FT-IR, $^1\mathsf{H}$ -NMR and $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ -CP-MAS-NMR studies of the solid or after acid digestion (Figure S20, S27 and S29). All experiments discard the incorporation of Cp units to the material for the synthetic conditions used. To further confirm this point, we calculated theoretically the formation energy of the cluster $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{T i}_{3}(\mu_{3^{-}}$ $\mathsf{O})\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{C p})_{2}(\mathsf{b t c})_{6}]$ (SI5). As shown in Figure S38, our calculations suggest that the incorporation of Cp linkers to axial positions in the Ti sites is thermodynamically disfavoured, for a difference in energy of 1.1 or $1.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ over the formation of ${\mathsf{O H}}^{}/{\mathsf{O}}^{2-}$ or $O^{2\cdot}$ $A_{2}O$ capped clusters.
This systematic exploration of conditions and metal precursors suggests that the formation of trimeric ${\sf T i}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\sf-O})$ clusters units for the formation of MIL-100(Ti) under optimized reaction conditions is thermodynamically favoured not only for preformed clusters but also for organometallic complexes. However, these last are less effective in preventing the formation of amorphous fractions of the material responsible for the observed reduction in porosity in MIL-100(Ti)- ${\cdot}\mathsf{C p}_{2}\mathsf{T i C l}_{2}$ . Overall, our experiments highlight the importance of controlling the reactivity of Ti precursors with water to enable the formation of crystalline, porous titanium-frameworks.
## Conclusions
The design of new titanium-frameworks is arguably limited by the high reactivity of ${\mathsf{T i}}(1\vee)$ with water and the subsequent difficulties in controlling the formation of persistent SBUs with the rigidity and directionality required to target specific architectures. We have shown how the use of high-throughput synthetic methodologies is useful to identify the conditions required to form ${\sf T i}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\sf-O})$ metal-oxo clusters from different titanium precursors. This classical SBU can be then reticulated into a MIL-100 topology by direct reaction with trimesic acid to produce a mesoporous material with excellent thermal and hydrolytical stability, linked to the incorporation of highly charged Ti(IV) atoms. MIL-100(Ti) also inherits the photoactivity of other titanium-frameworks for the generation of reactive Ti(III) species with light upon LMCT. In this regard, the combination of chemical stability and photoactivity into a mesoporous framework, also amenable to the generation of vacant sites, offers a promising route for the design of advanced porous photocatalysts without the metric limitations imposed by the microporosity of the materials currently available. Based on preliminary results, we are confident this MOF shall be also compatible with linker functionalization or metal doping for engineering its optical response with visible light. In the context of biomedical applications, the low-toxicity of titanium and the different degradation rates compared to MIL-100(Ti) might be also relevant for approaching targeted release of drugs.
## Experimental Section
Chemical and Physical Characterization: Infrared Spectra were recorded in an Agilent Cary 630 FTIR Spectrometer equipped with an ATR module. Thermogravimetric analysis was carried out with a Mettler Toledo TGA/SDTA 851e/SF/1100 between 25 and $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at a rate of $5^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ with a flow of ${\mathsf N}_{2}{:}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ (4:1). PXRD patterns were collected in a PANalytical X’Pert PRO diffractometer using copper radiation $(\mathsf{C u}\quad\mathsf{K}_{\alpha})$ with an X’Celerator detector, operating at $40~\mathsf{m A}$ and $45~\mathsf{k V}$ . Profiles were collected in the $2^{\circ}<2\theta<90^{\circ}$ range with a step size of 0.013. Particle morphologies and dimensions were studied with a Hitachi S-4800 scanning electron microscope operating at 20 kV over metalized samples over metallized samples. Surface area, pore size and volume values were calculated from nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms recorded at $77~\mathsf{K}$ on a Micromeritics 3Flex apparatus. Samples were degassed overnight at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $10^{-6}$ torr prior to analysis. Surface areas were estimated according to the BET model and pore size dimensions were calculated by Non-Linear Density Functional Theory model (NLDFT) for the adsorption branch assuming a cylindrical pore model. $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ -CP-MAS-NMR were carried out on a Bruker Avance III 400 WB Spectrometer. Samples were loaded in a $4\mathsf{m m}$ zirconia rotor and spun at 8kHz. $^1\mathsf{H}$ -NMR spectra were run on a Bruker DRX300 spectrometer. See SI for additional experimental details on digestion of the solids for NMR study.
Synthesis of $\left[\mathsf{T i}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6}(\mathsf{O}^{\mathrm{i}}\mathsf{P r})_{6}(4\mathsf{-t b b}z)_{6}\right](\mathsf{T i}_{6})$ : The synthesis of the Ti6 cluster was carried out according to the previously reported procedure.47
