Synthesis of MIL-100(Ti) from other Ti(IV) precursors $(\mathsf{C p}_{2}\mathsf{T i C l}_{2},$ $\mathsf{c p T i C l}_{3},$ $\pi(0^{\circ}\mathsf{P r})_{4})$ : The synthesis of MIL-100(Ti) from simple Ti(IV) precursors was carried out by adding $6.2~\mathsf{m g}$ of ${\mathsf{C p}}_{2}{\mathsf{T i C l}}_{2},$ $5.4~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{C p T i C l}_{3}$ or $7.2~\upmu\up L$ of ${\mathsf{T i}}({\mathsf{O}}^{\mathsf{i}}{\mathsf{P r}})_{4}$ $24\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of Ti(IV)), 25.0 mg of H3btc $(120~\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ to $3m L$ of a mixture of ACN:THF (3:1, $v/v\%)$ in a glass vial. Subsequently, $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of acetic acid were added and the mixture was sonicated to get a homogeneous suspension. The vial was placed in the oven at $160~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 12 hours. After cooling down to room temperature, the white microcrystalline powder was recovered by centrifugation, rinsed with fresh DMF and MeOH and further washed by soxhlet extraction with hot EtOH or MeOH for several hours. The solid was then allowed to dry under vacuum at room temperature.
